      "items": [
        {
          "handle": "@b328c94c44c_10",
          "kind": "block",
          "sourceAuthor": "human",
          "contribution": {
            "handle": "@c328c94c44c_24",
            "author": "human",
            "participant": {
              "id": "f4ce69a1-bfae-4770-902e-8f344cce1409:human",
              "kind": "human",
              "name": "Local author"
            },
            "ordinal": 7,
            "createdUtc": "2026-11-11T02:27:57.539Z"
          },
          "excerpt": "Here's my position: an object remains the same object as long as its history is continuous, even if none of its original material survives. Physical composition is irrelevant to identity.",
          "readRequired": true,
          "retrieval": "saved-semantic"
        },
        {
          "handle": "@b328c94c44c_25",
          "kind": "block",
          "sourceAuthor": "human",
          "contribution": {
            "handle": "@c328c94c44c_26",
            "author": "human",
            "participant": {
              "id": "f4ce69a1-bfae-4770-902e-8f344cce1409:human",
              "kind": "human",
              "name": "Local author"
            },
            "ordinal": 11,
            "createdUtc": "2026-11-11T02:32:23.898Z"
          },
          "excerpt": "I'm beginning to doubt my earlier claim that physical composition is irrelevant to identity. I think I was too categorical. How would you revise that position?",
          "readRequired": true,
          "retrieval": "saved-semantic"
        },
        {
          "handle": "@b328c94c44c_2",
          "kind": "block",
          "sourceAuthor": "human",
          "contribution": {
            "handle": "@c328c94c44c_1",
            "author": "human",
            "participant": {
              "id": "f4ce69a1-bfae-4770-902e-8f344cce1409:human",
              "kind": "human",
              "name": "Local author"
            },
            "ordinal": 20,
            "createdUtc": "2026-11-14T05:23:27.760Z"
          },
          "excerpt": "Use search_symposium to find my earlier positions about physical composition and identity, including my later doubt and your objections or qualifications. Read the relevant returned sources, then briefly compare how the position changed, citing the earlier contributions. Two paragraphs are enough.",
          "readRequired": true,
          "retrieval": "saved-semantic"
        },
        {
          "handle": "@b328c94c44c_27",
          "kind": "block",
          "sourceAuthor": "human",
          "contribution": {
            "handle": "@c328c94c44c_28",
            "author": "human",
            "participant": {
              "id": "f4ce69a1-bfae-4770-902e-8f344cce1409:human",
              "kind": "human",
              "name": "Local author"
            },
            "ordinal": 13,
            "createdUtc": "2026-11-11T02:33:13.996Z"
          },
          "excerpt": "Could we apply the same reasoning to a person? My body replaces many of its constituent cells over time, and my memories and personality also change. Yet I still regard myself as the same person. Is personal identity fundamentally different from the identity of an artefact?",
          "readRequired": true,
          "retrieval": "saved-semantic"
        },
        {
          "handle": "@b328c94c44c_18",
          "kind": "block",
          "sourceAuthor": "ai",
          "contribution": {
            "handle": "@c328c94c44c_29",
            "author": "ai",
            "participant": {
              "id": "f4ce69a1-bfae-4770-902e-8f344cce1409:ai",
              "kind": "ai",
              "name": "AI participant"
            },
            "ordinal": 8,
            "createdUtc": "2026-11-11T02:28:17.187Z",
            "replies": [
              "@c328c94c44c_24"
            ],
            "discursiveReferences": [
              "@r328c94c44c_30"
            ]
          },
          "excerpt": "I’m sympathetic to giving historical continuity priority, but I’d separate two claims: original material need not survive, and physical composition is irrelevant. The first doesn’t establish the second. Material and structural changes might affect whether an object survives, even if no particular plank is indispensable.",
          "readRequired": true,
          "retrieval": "saved-semantic"
        },
        {
          "handle": "@b328c94c44c_12",
          "kind": "block",
          "sourceAuthor": "ai",
          "contribution": {
            "handle": "@c328c94c44c_29",
            "author": "ai",
            "participant": {
              "id": "f4ce69a1-bfae-4770-902e-8f344cce1409:ai",
              "kind": "ai",
              "name": "AI participant"
            },
            "ordinal": 8,
            "createdUtc": "2026-11-11T02:28:17.187Z",
            "replies": [
              "@c328c94c44c_24"
            ],
            "discursiveReferences": [
              "@r328c94c44c_30"
            ]
          },
          "excerpt": "Consider a ship gradually consumed by fire. There’s an uninterrupted causal history connecting the vessel to the resulting ash, but we ordinarily say the ship was destroyed—not that it became a ship made of ash. That puts pressure on continuity alone as a sufficient condition for numerical identity: causal succession can connect an object to its remains without preserving the object.",
          "readRequired": true,
          "retrieval": "saved-semantic"
        },
        {
          "handle": "@b328c94c44c_31",
          "kind": "block",
          "sourceAuthor": "ai",
          "contribution": {
            "handle": "@c328c94c44c_29",
            "author": "ai",
            "participant": {
              "id": "f4ce69a1-bfae-4770-902e-8f344cce1409:ai",
              "kind": "ai",
              "name": "AI participant"
            },
            "ordinal": 8,
            "createdUtc": "2026-11-11T02:28:17.187Z",
            "replies": [
              "@c328c94c44c_24"
            ],
            "discursiveReferences": [
              "@r328c94c44c_30"
            ]
          },
          "excerpt": "Your view could distinguish identity-preserving continuity from mere causal succession. But then it needs an independent account of that distinction; otherwise “its history remains continuous” risks meaning “it continues to be the same object.” Preserving an organised whole through repairs is one possible answer, though that gives physical organisation a role again.",
          "readRequired": true,
          "retrieval": "saved-semantic"
        },
        {
          "handle": "@b328c94c44c_32",
          "kind": "block",
          "sourceAuthor": "ai",
          "contribution": {
            "handle": "@c328c94c44c_29",
            "author": "ai",
            "participant": {
              "id": "f4ce69a1-bfae-4770-902e-8f344cce1409:ai",
              "kind": "ai",
              "name": "AI participant"
            },
            "ordinal": 8,
            "createdUtc": "2026-11-11T02:28:17.187Z",
            "replies": [
              "@c328c94c44c_24"
            ],
            "discursiveReferences": [
              "@r328c94c44c_30"
            ]
          },
          "excerpt": "What, on your account, distinguishes replacing the ship’s planks from burning it—without already assuming which process preserves the ship?",
          "readRequired": true,
          "retrieval": "saved-semantic"
        },
        {
          "handle": "@b328c94c44c_33",
          "kind": "block",
          "sourceAuthor": "human",
          "contribution": {
            "handle": "@c328c94c44c_34",
            "author": "human",
            "participant": {
              "id": "f4ce69a1-bfae-4770-902e-8f344cce1409:human",
              "kind": "human",
              "name": "Local author"
            },
            "ordinal": 5,
            "createdUtc": "2026-11-11T02:27:19.458Z"
          },
          "excerpt": "I'm wondering whether the word 'same' is doing too much work here. Perhaps numerical identity and qualitative identity are quite different things. That seems like a distinction that could change the whole discussion.",
          "readRequired": true,
          "retrieval": "saved-semantic"
        },
        {
          "handle": "@b328c94c44c_35",
          "kind": "block",
          "sourceAuthor": "ai",
          "contribution": {
            "handle": "@c328c94c44c_36",
            "author": "ai",
            "participant": {
              "id": "f4ce69a1-bfae-4770-902e-8f344cce1409:ai",
              "kind": "ai",
              "name": "AI participant"
            },
            "ordinal": 6,
            "createdUtc": "2026-11-11T02:27:38.725Z",
            "replies": [
              "@c328c94c44c_34"
            ]
          },
          "excerpt": "Yes—that distinction separates being one and the same thing from being alike. It changes how we should frame the puzzle.",
          "readRequired": true,
          "retrieval": "saved-semantic"
        },
        {
          "handle": "@b328c94c44c_11",
          "kind": "block",
          "sourceAuthor": "ai",
          "contribution": {
            "handle": "@c328c94c44c_36",
            "author": "ai",
            "participant": {
              "id": "f4ce69a1-bfae-4770-902e-8f344cce1409:ai",
              "kind": "ai",
              "name": "AI participant"
            },
            "ordinal": 6,
            "createdUtc": "2026-11-11T02:27:38.725Z",
            "replies": [
              "@c328c94c44c_34"
            ]
          },
          "excerpt": "Numerical identity: there is just one individual, considered at different times or under different descriptions. A ship can acquire a new mast or colour without necessarily becoming another ship.",
          "readRequired": true,
          "retrieval": "saved-semantic"
        },
        {
          "handle": "@b328c94c44c_37",
          "kind": "block",
          "sourceAuthor": "ai",
          "contribution": {
            "handle": "@c328c94c44c_36",
            "author": "ai",
            "participant": {
              "id": "f4ce69a1-bfae-4770-902e-8f344cce1409:ai",
              "kind": "ai",
              "name": "AI participant"
            },
            "ordinal": 6,
            "createdUtc": "2026-11-11T02:27:38.725Z",
            "replies": [
              "@c328c94c44c_34"
            ]
          },
          "excerpt": "Qualitative identity: things share the same qualities—strictly, all relevant qualitative properties; in ordinary speech, often just the features we’re comparing. Two separately built ships might look exactly alike yet still be two ships.",
          "readRequired": true,
          "retrieval": "saved-semantic"
        }
      ],
