        "scalarPolicy": "Strings are bounded to 1024 characters; detailed source text remains in read_source.text. Unsupported metadata/attributes are omitted, not round-tripped.",
        "root": {
          "handle": "@c328c94c44c_29",
          "kind": "native",
          "text": "",
          "configuration": {},
          "children": [
            {
              "handle": "@b328c94c44c_18",
              "kind": "text",
              "text": "I’m sympathetic to giving historical continuity priority, but I’d separate two claims: original material need not survive, and physical composition is irrelevant. The first doesn’t establish the second. Material and structural changes might affect whether an object survives, even if no particular plank is indispensable.",
              "configuration": {},
              "annotations": [
                {
                  "id": "862791cb-0b73-4345-9b88-d2de55a5624e",
                  "type": "mutable/entity-reference",
                  "start": 26,
                  "end": 46,
                  "omission": "Specialised attributes are not projected; identifier/range retained",
                  "entityHandle": "@e328c94c44c_5"
                },
                {
                  "id": "51c9d995-fb82-4c62-abf9-26c24bc3a6ab",
                  "type": "style/bold",
                  "start": 87,
                  "end": 120
                },
                {
                  "id": "c41b3ebe-4e32-4e47-bc31-0f96722d2c02",
                  "type": "style/bold",
                  "start": 127,
                  "end": 160
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "handle": "@b328c94c44c_12",
              "kind": "text",
              "text": "Consider a ship gradually consumed by fire. There’s an uninterrupted causal history connecting the vessel to the resulting ash, but we ordinarily say the ship was destroyed—not that it became a ship made of ash. That puts pressure on continuity alone as a sufficient condition for numerical identity: causal succession can connect an object to its remains without preserving the object.",
              "configuration": {},
              "annotations": [
                {
                  "id": "f7a3b134-37f0-4dfe-b50e-998fbe1f0617",
                  "type": "mutable/entity-reference",
                  "start": 281,
                  "end": 298,
                  "omission": "Specialised attributes are not projected; identifier/range retained",
                  "entityHandle": "@e328c94c44c_3"
                },
                {
                  "id": "112ef338-dbf1-441e-b3e2-fb539cbf6599",
                  "type": "dialectic/range-anchor",
                  "start": 0,
                  "end": 385,
                  "omission": "Specialised attributes are not projected; identifier/range retained",
                  "anchorHandle": "@r328c94c44c_40"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "handle": "@b328c94c44c_31",
              "kind": "text",
              "text": "Your view could distinguish identity-preserving continuity from mere causal succession. But then it needs an independent account of that distinction; otherwise “its history remains continuous” risks meaning “it continues to be the same object.” Preserving an organised whole through repairs is one possible answer, though that gives physical organisation a role again.",
              "configuration": {},
              "annotations": [
                {
                  "id": "a241653b-4a9f-4549-8813-650e7ef965bd",
                  "type": "style/italics",
                  "start": 28,
                  "end": 57
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "handle": "@b328c94c44c_32",
              "kind": "text",
              "text": "What, on your account, distinguishes replacing the ship’s planks from burning it—without already assuming which process preserves the ship?",
              "configuration": {}
            }
          ],
          "omissions": [
            "Only selected persistent configuration is projected; internal and unsupported metadata omitted",
            "Unmapped native kind; original identity and structure retained"
          ]
        }
